    "q-117889": {
      "dom_id": "step-q117889",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268429/assets/6737c9597df5b28719a9b38f9bbefae03d9624e6d94b574a410a57000a48b562.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}(x+\\frac{2C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{2C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}(x+\\frac{2C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268429/assets/6438f318c33223abe9f3d2102e6f9f35bf2a8a6e887955ec9230d22e8294536a.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{2C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
