    "q-141983": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141983",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268429/assets/a40509b3b31acce76da67837e9605ba4efd0d588feab227d9354bc5bab0582af.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-2D?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}=5.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $-\\frac{3}{2}$ unit down. Hence, $D=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=5-2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})=5+3=8.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x)-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
